ADE-4 is a multivariate analysis and graphical display software package for
Macintosh and micro-computers running the Windows 95 and Windows NT
systems. A series of multivariate techniques permits to analyze several
types of data (e.g., quantitative, qualitative, presence/absence). The
examples included in ADE-4 for trial runs mainly deal with ecological
questions. However, people from other disciplines (e.g., chemometry,
sociology) and interested in the use of multivariate analyses should find
in ADE-4 software the most recent developments.

These linear ordination techniques start with the analysis of data sets
presented
under the form of only one array. The ADE-4 software performs four basic
multivariate
methods. Principal components analysis (PCA) devoted to quantitative
measurements, correspondence analysis (COA) to study contingency tables,
multiple correspondence analysis (MCA) to analyze qualitative variables,
and fuzzy correspondence analysis dedicated to the analysis of fuzzy coded
variables. If the table incorporates a priori groups of individuals, the
user can perform discriminant analysis and within/between groups analyses
starting from either PCA, COA, MCA or FCA analyses. Spatial data analyses
(local and global analyses based on local variance and spatial
autocorrelation) are also available.

When two arrays are simultaneously analyzed (e.g. species-environment
relationships), ADE-4 can perform analyses with respect to instrumental
variables (e.g., canonical
correspondence analysis (CCA)), co-inertia analysis, and partial least
squares (PLS)
regression technique. The simultaneous ordination of three arrays is
available under the name of RLQ analysis.

Finally, K-sets data tables can be analyzed with the STATIS, ACOM or AFCMUL
methods.

